{
  "$schema": "./theme-design-response.schema.json",
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "design": {
    "concept": "Ultra Dusk is built rather than picked: every neutral is one hue at twelve fixed lightnesses, every accent is a measured OKLCH coordinate, and no value is sampled from an existing theme. The ground is a drained lavender at 281° — a hue that also exists in the accent set, so the canvas is family with everything drawn on it instead of a neutral grey the colours merely sit on. The foreground drifts warm-neutral as it lightens, which keeps long reading sessions calm and leaves saturation available for meaning.",
    "paletteCharacter": "Neutral chroma runs 0.023 at the canvas and settles to 0.019 at text, with hue easing from 281° toward 68°. Eleven accents sit on the Frappé-measured band — chroma targeted per hue across a 1.55× spread rather than held constant, because equal chroma does not read as equal colour: reds and violets carry more of it, teal and yellow less. Each accent ships in two tiers, a vivid value for code surfaces and a darkened chrome tier that clears 4.5:1 on canvas and panel.",
    "surfaceHierarchy": "Six steps: bedrock (dialogs, popovers, the deepest overlay) → substrate (sidebars, gutters, tab strip, inputs) → base (editor canvas, terminal, active tab) → panel (bottom dock, status bar, headers) → ridge (hover) → crest (pressed, section headers). Dialogs deliberately sit below the canvas rather than above it, so an overlay reads by depth and border rather than by brightness, and stays legible over both dark code and light diffs. Structural borders are quiet — veil for hairlines inside a surface, rim for boundaries between hosts — and are never the same value as the focus accent.",
    "accentStrategy": "One focus accent (#c0abf5) carries focusBorder, workbench.focusedHostAccent, the caret, the active tab rule and the active line number, so keyboard focus is a single learnable colour and is always more salient than a structural border. Selection is that accent at 15% over canvas, which stays readable when the host loses focus; hover is a neutral elevation step instead, so hover and selection differ in kind rather than in strength. Nine states carry eight distinct hues: error ember 20°, deleted quartz 10°, warning brass 84°, modified copper 44°, success and added fern 134°, untracked jade 184°, information glacier 205°, hint cobalt 264° — no pair is separated by brightness alone. Persistent chrome is never a full-bleed accent fill; the status bar is panel with a hairline and earns identity from one small tinted item.",
    "syntaxStrategy": "Syntax uses the vivid tier on canvas, matching how Catppuccin treats its own code surfaces, while UI chrome uses the darkened tier — the two never mix on one surface. Hues are assigned by role, not by rotation: orchid for keywords and storage, fern for strings, copper for numbers and constants, azure for functions and calls, brass for types and classes, cobalt for namespaces and tags, glacier for properties and JSON keys, jade for operators, quartz for macros, decorators and language variables. Font style is used sparingly and structurally — italic for comments, parameters, imports, interfaces and decorators; bold reserved for headings, SQL keywords and invalid tokens; strikethrough only for deprecation. TypeScript keeps property, parameter and type separate; Rust separates macros from functions; SQL keywords stay bold against unstyled identifiers; JSON keys read as properties, not strings; Markdown carries six distinct roles."
  },
